# Quotes and blocks

## Quotes {#quote}

Lines that start with `> ` are a quote. Its content is blocks, as anywhere else.

:example
```
> A quote, on two lines,
> with **markup**.
>
> > And a quote inside.
```

To say whose words they are, write the quote as a `quote` callout, with options:

:example
```
> [!quote][author=Ada Lovelace, cite=Notes]
> The Analytical Engine weaves
> algebraical patterns.
```

`author`, `cite` (the work) and `url` (where it is) are all optional.

In Lua, [[elements#element|`nml.element`]] builds a `quote`, with the same three fields; its children are its content:

:example
```
:lua`nml.element("quote", {
  author = "Ada Lovelace",
  "Built in Lua.",
})`
```

## Callouts {#callout}

A callout is a quote that starts with its kind, `[!kind]`, and an optional title.

:example
```
> [!warning] Before you start
> Back up your files.

> [!tip]
> Callouts hold any blocks:
>  * lists,
>  * code, tables…
```

| Kind | For |
|:-----|:----|
| `note` | Something to know. |
| `tip` | A better way. |
| `important` | Something not to miss. |
| `warning` | A risk. |
| `caution` | A danger. |
| `todo` | What is left to do. |
| `quote` | A quotation, with its source. |

In Lua, a `callout` has its kind as `type`, and a `title`:

:example
```
:lua`nml.element("callout", {
  type = "note",
  title = "From Lua",
  "A callout built by a script.",
})`
```

## Layouts {#layout}

A layout arranges blocks: `:layout` and its name, the parts separated by `:next`, and `:end`.

:example
```
:layout split
The left part.
:next
 * The right
 * part.
:end
```

:example
```
:layout spoiler The answer
42.
:end
```

| Layout | Parts | |
|:-------|:------|:-|
| `split` | Any number | Side by side. |
| `centered` | One | Centered. |
| `spoiler` | One | Hidden until the reader opens it; the title is what shows. |
| `sidebar` | One | Beside the page's content, in its sidebar, when it is not inside another block. |

`:end layout` may close a layout too, to make clear what ends where.

The sidebar is how a site adds navigation: this documentation's pages include a file with a `:layout sidebar` that holds a [[std#nav|menu]].

In Lua, a `layout` has its name as `type`, and a `title`; each child is a part:

:example
```
:lua`nml.element("layout", {
  type = "split",
  "The left part.",
  nml.element("bold", {
    "The right part.",
  }),
})`
```

A [[rules#|rule]] that writes a layout with `out:element` can write its last part with a function, as this documentation's `:example` does.

## Raw HTML {#raw}

`:raw` and a fenced block writes HTML to the page as it is; ``:raw`…` `` does it in a line.

:example
````
:raw
```
<hr>
```

A line:raw`<br>`break.
````

NML does not check raw HTML: it is for what NML cannot write. Text that a [[lua#|script]] returns is always escaped; scripts write HTML with a `raw` element.

In Lua, a `raw` element's `content` is the HTML; `block` writes it as a block rather than in a line:

:example
```
:lua`nml.element("raw", {
  content = "<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>",
})`
```
